module Core

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   A string as its own source text - the escapes, and the reader that takes them back off.
   ==========================================================================================

   `Show(String)` writes a string as itself, which is what a format hole wants and is exactly wrong
   for the three places a string is quoted: a diagnostic naming the text it read, a JSON-ish emitter,
   and any "show me the raw text" where a newline has to arrive as two units rather than as a line
   break. Every one of those was open-coding the same five tests, or not doing it at all.

   **The escape set is JSON's**, and that is a decision rather than an accident: `\"`, `\\`, `\b`,
   `\f`, `\n`, `\r`, `\t` and `\uXXXX` for anything else below a space. It is the set every other
   notation is a subset of - a C string literal, a Yana one, a shell `$'...'` - so text this writes
   is text those readers accept, where a set of this library's own would have been one nobody else
   parses. `\uXXXX` is the only escape that is not two units, and it is only reached by a control
   unit, which is what makes the common case a copy.

   **Units at or above a space are written through untouched, including the ones above 127.** A
   `CodeUnit` is one unit of the target's own encoding - a UTF-8 byte natively and a UTF-16 unit on
   JS - so `é` written from a native build would be spelling half of an `é` rather than the
   character, and would read back on the other target as something else. JSON permits raw UTF-8 in a
   string, so passing them through is both the correct answer and the portable one; what it costs is
   that this cannot produce pure-ASCII output, which is a job for a transcoder rather than for an
   escape table.

   The reading direction does accept `\uXXXX` above 127, because a text this did not write may carry
   one - so there is a code-point encoder here, and it is the one part of this file that is split by
   target.
-}

{-
   How to quote.

   One setting, and it is which unit delimits: `"` is JSON's and is the default, and `'` is what a
   diagnostic quoting an identifier wants. Whichever it is gets a backslash in front of it inside the
   text and the other one does not, so `quoted(text, quote: "'")` writes `'he said "hi"'` with one
   escape rather than three.

   A scalar, like every other style in this directory, for `Core/Format.yana`'s reason: provenance is
   field-insensitive, so a style that held a reference would make every `Formatted` over it a root of
   one.
-}
pub data QuoteStyle {
    quote: CodeUnit = "\""
}

pub alias QuotedFormat = Formatted(QuoteStyle, String)

pub fn quoteStyle(quote: CodeUnit = "\"") -> QuoteStyle = QuoteStyle {quote: quote}

-- The formatter, written as a record literal rather than through `with` for the reason
-- `Core/Format.yana` states: `with` takes its style as `'s`, and a constructor building its own
-- style would be handing back a root into its own frame.
pub fn quoted(value: 'String, quote: CodeUnit = "\"") -> QuotedFormat =
    Formatted {value: value, style: quoteStyle(quote)}

-- `\uXXXX`, which is the escape for everything the short spellings do not cover. Lower case, because
-- that is what every emitter writes and what `hexUnitCased` answers by default; a reader takes
-- either.
fn pushUnitEscape(&to: String, unit: Int):
    String.pushUnit(to, "\\")
    String.pushUnit(to, "u")
    String.pushUnit(to, hexUnitCased((unit `shr` 12) `and` 15, False))
    String.pushUnit(to, hexUnitCased((unit `shr` 8) `and` 15, False))
    String.pushUnit(to, hexUnitCased((unit `shr` 4) `and` 15, False))
    String.pushUnit(to, hexUnitCased(unit `and` 15, False))

-- The two-unit escapes, as the letter that follows the backslash, or zero for a unit that has none.
-- A table rather than a `match` chain would be six loads to save five compares over a range the
-- branch predictor sees once per string.
fn shortEscape(unit: Int) -> Int:
    if unit == "\b" then return "b"
    if unit == "\t" then return "t"
    if unit == "\n" then return "n"
    if unit == "\f" then return "f"
    if unit == "\r" then return "r"
    return 0

instance ShowWith(QuoteStyle, String):
    fn showWith(subject: QuotedFormat, &to: String):
        let quote = subject.style.quote :: Int
        let text = subject.value
        let units = length(text)

        String.pushUnit(to, quote)

        for i in 0 .. units:
            let unit = String.unit(text, i)
            let short = shortEscape(unit)

            if unit == quote || unit == "\\":
                String.pushUnit(to, "\\")
                String.pushUnit(to, unit)
            else if short != 0:
                String.pushUnit(to, "\\")
                String.pushUnit(to, short)
            -- Everything else below a space, and the delete unit, which is unprintable and is what
            -- a terminal would act on rather than show.
            else if unit < " " || unit == "\x7f" then pushUnitEscape(to, unit)
            else String.pushUnit(to, unit)

        String.pushUnit(to, quote)

    {-
       No bound, and that is the honest answer rather than a missing one.

       What a bound may not be is short, so the only true constant here is `2 + 6 * length` - the
       whole text turning out to be control units - and seeding a buffer at six times the text is a
       worse trade than growing it: a quoted string is nearly always its own units plus two, and the
       escapes that make it longer are rare enough that no program should reserve for them.

       `formatBound` turns this into a seed of zero and the appends grow past it, which is
       Implementation-Storage.md part 8's strategy (c) reached the way `Aligned` reaches it.
    -}
    fn showBoundWith(subject: QuotedFormat) -> Maybe(Size) = Nothing

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Reading one back
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   The dual, and the same style: `readWith(quoteStyle(), text, at)` reads what `quoted(text)` wrote,
   which is the property every pair in this directory has and the reason a style is a record rather
   than an argument list per direction.

   **The two failures are the two `ReadError` means.** A text that does not begin with the quote unit
   is `NoDigits` - "this is not one of these, try something else", which is what a parser choosing
   between a string and a number needs. A text that begins with one and does not end with one, or
   that carries an escape this does not know, is `OutOfRange`: it *is* a quoted string and it is
   wrong, and a caller should blame the input rather than back out. The cursor does not move in
   either case.

   **The result is owned**, which is what a reader answers and a formatter cannot: there is nothing
   to borrow, so the units go into a new `String` and the caller has it outright. Sized from the
   text's own length, which is exact for a string with no escapes and generous by the escapes' own
   shrinkage for one with them - a `\uXXXX` is six units in and at most three out.
-}
instance ReadWith(QuoteStyle, String):
  fn readWith(style: QuoteStyle, from: String, &at: Size) -> Result(ReadError, String):
    let quote = style.quote :: Int
    let units = length(from)

    if at >= units || String.unit(from, at) != quote then return Err(NoDigits)

    let &cursor = at + 1
    let &->text = String.ofCapacity(units - cursor)

    while cursor < units:
        let unit = String.unit(from, cursor)

        if unit == quote:
            at = cursor + 1
            return Ok(text)

        if unit != "\\":
            String.pushUnit(text, unit)
            cursor = cursor + 1
            continue

        -- An escape, whose letter is the unit after the backslash. A backslash at the very end is a
        -- text that ended inside an escape, which is the unterminated case a unit early.
        if cursor + 1 >= units then return Err(OutOfRange)

        let letter = String.unit(from, cursor + 1)
        cursor = cursor + 2

        match letter:
            "b" -> String.pushUnit(text, "\b")
            "f" -> String.pushUnit(text, "\f")
            "n" -> String.pushUnit(text, "\n")
            "r" -> String.pushUnit(text, "\r")
            "t" -> String.pushUnit(text, "\t")
            "/" -> String.pushUnit(text, "/")
            "u":
                -- `\uXXXX`, which is exactly four digits: fewer is not a shorter escape but a
                -- malformed one, since nothing says where it would stop.
                if cursor + 4 > units then return Err(OutOfRange)

                let &point = 0
                for digit in 0 .. 4:
                    match hexValue(String.unit(from, cursor + digit)):
                        Just(value) -> point = (point `shl` 4) `or` (truncate(value) :: Int)
                        Nothing -> return Err(OutOfRange)

                cursor = cursor + 4
                pushCodePoint(text, point)
            _:
                -- The quote unit, the backslash and anything else this file wrote itself arrive
                -- here as themselves, which is what makes `\"` and `\\` need no arm of their own.
                -- An unknown letter is refused rather than passed through: `\q` is a text somebody
                -- got wrong, and reading it as `q` would hide that.
                if letter != quote && letter != "\\" then return Err(OutOfRange)
                String.pushUnit(text, letter)

    -- The units ran out before the closing quote did.
    return Err(OutOfRange)

pub fn readQuoted(from: String, &at: Size, quote: CodeUnit = "\"") -> Result(ReadError, String) =
    readWith(quoteStyle(quote), from, at)

{-
   One code point, in the target's own encoding - the one thing in this file that is not one body.

   `\uXXXX` names a *code point* and a `String` holds *code units*, and those are the same number
   only below 128. On JS they are the same thing again up to `0xFFFF`, because a host string is
   UTF-16 and `\uXXXX` is exactly one of its units; natively the point has to be encoded, and the
   two or three bytes it becomes are what a UTF-8 string holds.

   No surrogate handling, because `\uXXXX` cannot name a point above `0xFFFF`: a text that carries
   an astral character as a surrogate pair reads here as the two points it wrote, which is what every
   decoder that does not re-pair them does. Re-pairing is a decoder's job and this is an escape.
-}
@platform(native) fn pushCodePoint(&to: String, point: Int):
    if point < 128 then String.pushUnit(to, point)
    else if point < 2048:
        String.pushUnit(to, 192 `or` (point `shr` 6))
        String.pushUnit(to, 128 `or` (point `and` 63))
    else:
        String.pushUnit(to, 224 `or` (point `shr` 12))
        String.pushUnit(to, 128 `or` ((point `shr` 6) `and` 63))
        String.pushUnit(to, 128 `or` (point `and` 63))

@platform(js) fn pushCodePoint(&to: String, point: Int): String.pushUnit(to, point)
